WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.897 --> 00:00:09.036
BEN: Hey there, Little Fox readers, and welcome back to People in the News . Today Olivia is going

2
00:00:09.037 --> 00:00:15.365
to report on Steven Spielberg, one of the world's greatest filmmakers. OLIVIA: Steven was born on December 18, 1946,

3
00:00:15.366 --> 00:00:23.126
and he saw his first movie when he was about six years old. It featured a huge train

4
00:00:23.127 --> 00:00:28.108
crash, which Steven thought was really cool. Attempting to recreate the dramatic crash, Steven borrowed his father's home movie

5
00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:37.697
camera and filmed his own model trains crashing into each other. As a kid Steven went on to make

6
00:00:37.698 --> 00:00:44.506
more short films, using his sisters and friends as actors. He didn't have money for special effects, so he

7
00:00:44.507 --> 00:00:52.761
had to invent creative ways to simulate explosions and airplanes in flight. When Steven saw how much his

8
00:00:52.762 --> 00:01:02.088
friends and family enjoyed watching his finished movies, he knew he wanted to spend the rest of his life

9
00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:13.156
making films. At the age of 16, Steven borrowed $500 from his father and made his first full-length movie,

10
00:01:13.157 --> 00:01:24.248
Firelight , about aliens arriving on Earth. A local theater owner agreed to show it one night, and, through

11
00:01:24.249 --> 00:01:28.317
ticket sales, Steven earned back the $500 he had borrowed, and he even made a profit of one

12
00:01:28.318 --> 00:01:37.493
dollar! But Steven wanted to learn more about making real movies. So one day, while he was on a

13
00:01:37.494 --> 00:01:45.802
tour of a film studio, he sneaked away from the tour group and spent the rest of the day

14
00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:56.034
snooping around the lot. Steven returned each day that summer, saying "hi" to the security guard on duty, and

15
00:01:56.035 --> 00:02:00.453
pretending to be a film studio employee. He was able to watch some famous Hollywood directors as they

16
00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:06.522
worked, and he met film and sound editors, learning more about the technical aspects of filmmaking. Steven planned to

17
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:13.291
enroll in film school after high school, of course. But unfortunately he wasn't able to get into any colleges

18
00:02:13.292 --> 00:02:17.932
that offered film programs. BEN: That doesn't make sense. He was obviously talented, so why couldn't he get into

19
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:22.436
a film school? OLIVIA: Even though Steven is a smart guy, his grades were terrible. He had neglected his

20
00:02:22.437 --> 00:02:26.941
schoolwork because he was so busy making movies! Steven was not about to give up his dream of

21
00:02:26.942 --> 00:02:31.016
becoming a filmmaker though. He continued to make short films and hang around the film studio. Finally, one of

22
00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:38.778
his movies caught the attention of some executives, and Steven was offered a job directing television shows. He proved

23
00:02:38.779 --> 00:02:44.427
to be very talented, and was soon promoted to directing movies. Eventually Steven was offered the opportunity to direct

24
00:02:44.428 --> 00:02:54.166
Jaws , a thriller about an enormous, bloodthirsty shark that terrorizes beachgoers. Steven told the film's producers that

25
00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:59.419
he didn't want to show the shark too much. He believed Jaws would be a lot scarier if he

26
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:05.575
just focused on the water and used scary music. The producers said no, but Steven wound up getting his

27
00:03:05.576 --> 00:03:09.987
way. That was because the mechanical sharks he was using kept sinking; he had to shoot most of the

28
00:03:09.988 --> 00:03:17.318
movie without them anyway! Jaws was released in the summer of 1975, and Steven's technique worked; audiences were

29
00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:26.058
terrified by the movie. In fact, many American beach towns reported a decline in tourism that summer because so

30
00:03:26.059 --> 00:03:35.552
many people were now afraid to go in the ocean! Jaws made more money than any other movie at

31
00:03:35.553 --> 00:03:50.608
that time, and its success gave birth to a new trend in Hollywood: the summer blockbuster—that's a fast-paced, entertaining

32
00:03:50.609 --> 00:04:00.024
movie accompanied by lots of merchandise and advertising. Steven went on to direct many more blockbusters. E.T.: The

33
00:04:00.025 --> 00:04:06.429
Extra-Terrestrial is about a young boy who befriends an alien who is stranded on Earth. The action-packed Indiana Jones

34
00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:15.500
movies follow the adventures of a treasure-hunting archaeologist. Steven also made Jurassic Park , a scary movie about a

35
00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:22.753
scientist who discovers a way to clone dinosaurs and bring them back to life. That film used cutting-edge computer

36
00:04:22.754 --> 00:04:33.669
technology to depict very realistic-looking dinosaurs. Despite the commercial success of many of Steven's movies, some film critics believed

37
00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.824
he could only make action movies with fancy special effects, not serious films. BEN: Did that bother Steven?

38
00:04:35.825 --> 00:04:44.737
OLIVIA: Yes, but he soon had an opportunity to prove those critics wrong with Schindler's List, a film that

39
00:04:44.738 --> 00:04:53.563
came out late in 1993. It was based on the true story of a factory owner who saved the

40
00:04:53.564 --> 00:05:01.056
lives of about 1,100 Jews during the Holocaust in World War II. The movie looks nothing like Steven's other

41
00:05:01.057 --> 00:05:09.134
movies; it is in black and white, and it contains no special effects at all. It was a

42
00:05:09.135 --> 00:05:15.794
critical success, winning lots of awards and establishing him as a director who could handle serious topics. And get

43
00:05:15.795 --> 00:05:20.703
this, Ben: Even though Schindler's List made a lot of money, Steven didn't keep any of it. Instead he

44
00:05:20.704 --> 00:05:28.457
used the film's profits to establish two foundations: Survivors of the Shoah Visual History Foundation, which records video testimonies

45
00:05:28.458 --> 00:05:36.211
from Holocaust survivors, and the Righteous Persons Foundation, which provides grant money to organizations dedicated to promoting Jewish

46
00:05:36.212 --> 00:05:44.100
identity. Since then, Steven has continued to make a wide variety of movies, including The Adventures of Tintin and

47
00:05:44.101 --> 00:05:50.671
War Horse . Steven doesn't just direct movies; he's also involved in the business side of the film industry.

48
00:05:50.672 --> 00:06:01.008
Through his two production companies, Amblin Entertainment and Dreamworks Studios, he has produced quite a few highly successful movies

49
00:06:01.009 --> 00:06:09.835
from other directors. Some of Steven's movies are serious and explore complicated subjects, but others are more like

50
00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:16.573
his early movies—good, plain fun! BEN: Thanks, Olivia. It sounds like Steven hasn't changed much since he was a

51
00:06:16.574 --> 00:06:23.970
kid; he still loves to entertain people. Thanks for tuning in, Little Fox readers. See you all next week!

